# GetWorkspace.srv — read the planner's collision workspace (the Mapper
# TSDF/ESDF grid extent), the curobo analogue of MoveIt's Context-tab
# "Workspace" group.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   cuRobo has no planning database, but it does have ONE well-defined
#   workspace: the Mapper's TSDF/ESDF grid, a box of size `extent` centred at
#   `center` in the robot config's base frame. Everything the cameras can
#   observe — and therefore every voxel that becomes collision geometry for the
#   solvers — lives inside that box, and the wireframe published on
#   <node>/mapper_workspace draws exactly it. This service reports the two
#   numbers the launch file used to be the only home for, so a UI can show and
#   edit them instead of reading a YAML.
#
# READ-ONLY AND CHEAP
#   Touches no solver and rebuilds nothing (it reads the values in force), so it
#   is safe to poll. A size change (SetWorkspace) is NOT cheap: it rebuilds the
#   Mapper and every solver, like set_collision_cache.

# --- Request (empty: pure query) ---
---

# --- Response ---
bool success
string message

# Frame the box is expressed in (the robot config's kinematics.base_link).
string frame_id

# Box centre, [x, y, z] in frame_id (the launch file's `mapper_grid_center`).
# Always reported, even with no Mapper: the box is what the
# `mapper_workspace` wireframe draws and what a later Mapper would be built on,
# so it is a real, configured value — not a made-up default.
float64[] center

# Box size, [x, y, z] in frame_id, metres (the launch file's
# `mapper_extent_xyz`). Always reported; see `center`.
float64[] extent

# ESDF voxel size (m) the grid is rasterised at; the collision world cannot
# resolve detail finer than one voxel (the launch file's `voxel_size`).
float64 voxel_size

# True when a Mapper actually exists (a camera or laser feeds it). When false
# the server plans against analytic obstacles only: `center`/`extent` still
# describe the drawn box, but a workspace edit moves the box and its marker
# without touching any collision geometry — the panel says so rather than
# pretending the edit did something.
bool perception_active
